## Statement

The parent representatives are used exactly, with the same construction as the
sibling runner:

```text
K0: phi=+1, representative t == 1 (scalar tight-binding; extensive zero surface).
K1: phi=-1, representative eta0: eta0_1 = 1, eta0_2 = (-1)^{x1}, eta0_3 = (-1)^{x1+x2}
(Kawamoto-Smit class; 8 isolated Dirac zeros; = absorbed naive Dirac).
```

For each lattice direction `mu`, let `B_mu(K)` be the unital `*`-algebra
generated on the one-site qubit factor by the computed direction coefficient
of the representative `K`. The sibling note verifies that `B_mu(K0) = C * I`
and that `B_mu(K1)` is the maximal abelian algebra generated by the computed
self-adjoint unitary `Gamma_mu`.

On the two-class surface, the nearest-neighbor availability map induced by a
kinetic representative assigns, per site `x`, direction `mu`, and neighbor
qubit content `s`, an available-possibility subset `A_mu(s)`: the one-site pure
states compatible with the direction coefficient structure. For K1 this is the
eigenspace condition of the direction-tagged algebra:

```text
P_mu(lambda) = (I + lambda Gamma_mu) / 2,  lambda in {+1, -1},
A_mu(lambda) = {pure rays psi : P_mu(lambda) psi = psi}.
```

The neighbor's locked eigenvalue selects the projector. For K0, the algebra is
`C * I`, so its only spectral projector is `I` and the only covariant
availability map realized by the coefficient structure is the constant
full-subset map. A neighbor-conditioned record at `(x, mu)` is a record whose
locked possibility's availability differs across neighbor contents:
`A_mu(s) != A_mu(s')` for some `s, s'`.

**T-A - K0 vacuity, exact.** Every `U(1)`-frame-covariant availability map
realized by K0's nearest-neighbor coefficient structure is neighbor-constant:
the per-direction algebra `C * I` contains no nontrivial projector, and the
blocked-cell invariant-subspace computation has no trace-zero fixed direction.
No neighbor-conditioned record is available on K0, because the locking clause
never sees a neighbor-dependent subset.

**T-B - K1 realization, constructive.** K1's direction-tagged maximal abelian
algebras supply an explicit covariant neighbor-conditioned availability map,
the `Gamma_mu` eigen-projectors per direction. On a finite lattice, the runner
computes the projectors, checks they are rank-one and unequal, and exhibits a
witness record whose available subset changes when the neighbor locked
eigenvalue changes.

**T-C - conditional registration.** Therefore, pointwise at any supplied
realized state whose record stack contains at least one neighbor-conditioned
qubit record, the realized kinetic class is K1. By the sibling note's D1/D2
and the parent absorbing-frame theorem, the nonzero first-order Dirac-square
kinetic carrier with the mutually anticommuting self-adjoint-unitary
coefficient family follows. The branch is registered by the realized record
stack, not forced by the axioms.

## Proof Sketch

The runner reuses the sibling K0/K1 phase systems and absorbing-frame
coefficient extraction. T1 recomputes the flux anchor on `L = 4` and `L = 6`,
then verifies that K0 gives scalar direction coefficients while K1 gives the
same reconstructed `sum_mu Gamma_mu sin(p_mu)` symbol.

T-A is checked two ways. First, the spectral decomposition of each K0 direction
coefficient has only the full projector `I`. Second, the orbit/nullspace
computation for the degenerate K0 qubit factor has fixed Hermitian dimension
1 and trace-zero fixed dimension 0. Thus there is no nontrivial projector that
can be selected by a neighbor label from K0's coefficient structure.

T-B computes the K1 direction projectors from the extracted `Gamma_mu`, verifies
that each direction has two rank-one orthogonal projectors, and constructs
`A_mu(+1)` and `A_mu(-1)` with nonzero projector distance. The same projector
sets survive the allowed local `U(1)` frame phase, so the witness is covariant
in the parent sense.

T-C evaluates a supplied realized-state record stack pointwise: if it contains
a neighbor-conditioned qubit record, T-A excludes K0 while T-B realizes K1.
The same computed `Gamma_mu` family verifies the Dirac-square and
anticommutation consequences already isolated by the sibling note.

Three refutation legs guard the implication. T-D tries to build a covariant
neighbor-conditioned map on K0 and finds only the constant map. T-E shows that
an externally chosen conditioned basis map on K0 can be written down, but it is
a frame artifact rather than a map realized by `C * I`. T-F flips one eta0 link:
the object has mixed plaquette flux and the blocked coefficient structure fails
the K1 anticommutation discriminator.

## Consequence And Residual

The implication is conditional only. It does not assert that the four axioms
produce a record stack, and it does not decide whether Admissibility alone must
be read nonvacuously on the qubit factor. It uses the exact axiom clauses
"for each site, the available possibilities are determined by, and vary with,
the nearest-neighbor conditions" and "locks exactly one admissible local
possibility" only after a supplied realized state contains the relevant
record.

The parent residual remains intact: "the flux(-1) selector is not forced because K0 also satisfies the constraints". This note registers K1 from a
realized neighbor-conditioned record stack; it does not select the branch from
the axioms alone and leaves the selector undecided.

The next path this opens is only the conditional registration route: realized
cross-site records can identify the kinetic branch when they contain the
neighbor-conditioned pattern. Record formation, occurrence, probability, and
the Admissibility-reading decision remain separate downstream questions.

